Git Merge vs Git Rebase

Key Difference
Merge → combines branches and keeps history
Rebase → moves your branch and rewrites history

--------------------------------------------------
Visual Difference
Merge
main:     A---B---C-------M
                 \      /
feature:          D----E

Creates a merge commit (M)

Rebase
main:     A---B---C---D'---E'
 No merge commit, linear history
-------------------------------------------------

Comparison Table

Feature	     Merge 	                   Rebase 
History	    Preserved (non-linear)	   Rewritten (linear)
Extra       Commit	                   Yes (merge commit)	No
Safe	      Yes	                       Risky (if shared branch)
Use Case	  Team collaboration	       Clean commit history
Complexity	Easy	                   Slightly advanced
-------------------------------------------------------------------

1.Commands

a) Merge
git checkout main
git merge feature

b) Rebase
git checkout feature
git rebase main

Note : Important Rule

Never rebase shared/public branches

Simple Understanding
Merge = “join branches”
Rebase = “move my changes on top of latest branch”

Note: When to Use What?
---> Use Merge when:
Working in a team
You want full history
Safe and simple

---> Use Rebase when:
Working on your own branch
You want clean history

